<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/docs/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
import Query from './Query.abide'
import Regions from './Regions.abide'
import Tiles from './Tiles.abide'
import averageSource from './Average.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import querySource from './Query.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import regionSource from './Region.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import regionsSource from './Regions.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import tilesSource from './Tiles.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import totalSource from './Total.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="The two forms, and which one caches">
        <p>There is a cache at all because the body takes args, and the discriminant is exactly
        that.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>What you get</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>reactive(() =&gt; …)</code></td><td>tracked, one value, rerun by what it read</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>reactive((args) =&gt; …)</code></td><td><strong>untracked</strong>, one entry per args key, held</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>A keyed value does not rerun because something its body read moved — its ways back are
        <code>ttl</code>, an explicit <code>invalidate</code> or <code>refresh</code>, and eviction
        (11.6, 14.4). Its <code>ttl</code> is the life of an entry, counted from that entry's latest
        production (11.45, 11.49). The two are different shapes rather than one shape with an
        option, and every signature is in the
        <a href={url('/reference/reactive')}><code>reactive</code> reference</a>.</p>

        <p>Calling it hands back a <code>Reactive</code> for that key, so a probe on the result asks
        about that entry: <code>{'metricsFor({ range }).busy()'}</code>. The factory carries
        <code>busy</code> and <code>ready</code> too, and <code>metricsFor.busy()</code> asks about
        every entry, or about those a partial args matches (13.2, 13.11, 13.12). A handler declared
        over one carries the same two, so <code>{'getMetrics.busy({ range })'}</code> on the
        <code>GET</code> is the same question as <code>{'metricsFor.busy({ range })'}</code> on the
        value behind it.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A keyed value's cache is one entry per args key">
        <Example
            id="cache-shared"
            title="`reactive((args) => …)` — identity per set of arguments"
            summary="Two tiles on one dashboard, each on its own range. Put them both on 30d, then send one back to 7d."
            files={{
                'Tiles.abide': tilesSource,
                'Total.abide': totalSource,
                'Average.abide': averageSource,
            }}
        >
            <Tiles />
        </Example>

        <p><em>1 request, 2 readers</em> — against lifting the fetch to a common parent and
        threading it back down.</p>

        <p>Neither tile can reach the other, and the range each is on is the whole of what decides
        whether they share. On one range they are one entry, so the second tile to arrive there
        paints with no load and the record's own count stays where it was; on two ranges they are
        two entries and each pays for its own.</p>

        <p>The first paint is the case a hand-written table usually gets wrong, and it is the one
        the reader never causes: both tiles ask on the same tick, so the second gets <strong>the
        load already in flight</strong>. Holding the answer stops a second load only once the first
        has landed. What goes into the table has to be the entry, put there before the answer
        arrives.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/derive-a-value-from-other-values')}>Derived values</a> ·
        <a href={url('/values/decide-when-a-value-reloads')}>Reloading</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Two reads share an entry when their args canonicalize the same">
        <Example
            id="cache-canonical"
            title="One entry per canonical args key"
            summary="One panel, and three ways to spell the query behind it. Change the spelling, then change the region."
            files={{
                'Query.abide': querySource,
            }}
        >
            <Query />
        </Example>

        <p>Three spellings and one entry: the first two differ in argument order, the third writes
        out an option that is absent. None of them is a second request, and the region beside them
        is what the same rule looks like from the other side — a value that genuinely differs is a
        key that genuinely differs, and coming back to <code>north</code> is the entry still
        held.</p>

        <p>That is the cache's whole question, and the answer is the wire. <code>Args</code> is a
        <code>Record&lt;string, JsonValue&gt;</code>, serializable by contract, because <strong>the
        key is the wire form</strong>. An args object becomes a key by its canonical serialization,
        which is the same canonicalization the transport uses.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>The key it lands on</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'{ id: \'42\' }'}</code></td><td><code>{'{"id":"42"}'}</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'{ page: 2, id: \'42\' }'}</code></td><td><code>{'{"id":"42","page":2}'}</code> — sorted, so argument order never splits an entry</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'{ id: \'42\', after: undefined }'}</code></td><td><code>{'{"id":"42"}'}</code> — dropped, so an absent option is the same entry as an omitted one</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'{ since: new Date(0) }'}</code></td><td><code>{'{"since":"1970-01-01T00:00:00.000Z"}'}</code> — ISO</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>One canonicalization rather than two: args that cannot be keyed are exactly args that
        cannot be sent, so both fail in the same place. The key is therefore
        <strong>structural</strong>, and <code>{'reactive(() => getMetrics({ range }))'}</code>
        builds a fresh object on every run and still lands on the same entry — which is what makes
        the identity cutoff hold, so a recompute onto a key already held wakes nobody and shows no
        spinner.</p>

        <p>Args are always plain args. A reactive value in an argument position
        <strong>reads</strong>; making the call follow that value is the enclosing derived value's
        job.</p>

        <p>An <code>args</code> schema never decides the key. The key is taken before it runs
        (11.26), so it never moves a call onto another entry (11.28), and the body gets what it
        returned (11.68).</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A keyed value's cache is request-local on a server, process-local in a browser">
        <p>One scope is one caller on both sides, and only what counts as a caller differs.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th></th><th>server</th><th>browser</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>a caller is</td><td>one request</td><td>the one person at the tab</td></tr>
                <tr><td>the cache is built</td><td>when the request arrives</td><td>when the app starts</td></tr>
                <tr><td>and dropped</td><td>when the response is sent</td><td>when the tab closes</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ttl: Infinity</code> means</td><td>to the end of that request</td><td>to the end of that session</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>It follows that it is built and dropped with its scope. <code>ttl: Infinity</code> means
        to the end of that scope rather than forever, the cache cannot grow without bound, and one
        caller's answer can never be served to another. Coalescing is within a scope too: two calls
        inside one request share one load; two concurrent requests are two scopes and load
        twice.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`global` shares one cache across callers">
        <p><code>global: true</code> opts into a process-wide cache that outlives every request, and
        it is the only thing that makes two <strong>callers</strong> one load — which is its job
        where the load is expensive. No card on this page shows it, because a card is one browser
        tab and the thing <code>global</code> changes is what a second caller gets.</p>

        <p>It also decides what the answer says about caching. An rpc answer is <code>private,
        no-store</code> by default; a <code>GET</code> over a global keyed value answers
        <code>private, max-age=&lt;the ttl&gt;</code> instead, because those are one number and
        writing it twice in two units is how they come apart.</p>

        <p><strong>The directive stays <code>private</code>, and widening it is yours.</strong>
        <code>global</code> guarantees the answer does not vary by caller, a global body being
        unable to read the request scope. Whether a <strong>shared</strong> cache may hold it is a
        different question — about who may reach the endpoint rather than about what it returns —
        and abide cannot read that off a middleware rung. An endpoint that really is open says
        <code>public</code> in its own <code>ResponseInit</code>.</p>

        <p>Two costs, and both are yours rather than abide's.</p>

        <p><strong>A global body may not read the request scope.</strong> <code>request()</code>,
        <code>principal</code>, <code>cookies()</code>, <code>csp.nonce()</code> and
        <code>route</code> are a build error inside one, and the error names the ambient. An entry
        outlives the request that built it and is served to every caller after it, so a request
        ambient in the body bakes the first caller's request into everybody's answer. Anything
        caller-specific belongs in the <code>Args</code> instead, where it keys an entry rather than
        hiding in one. That refusal also lets the <code>cache-control</code> be derived rather than
        trusted: the answer is not about who asked because the shape that would make it so does not
        compile.</p>

        <p>And a global value is bounded by its own <code>ttl</code> and the app's
        <code>invalidate</code> and by nothing else. There is no byte ceiling and no eviction count:
        measuring an arbitrary value costs an O(size) walk, and an LRU would evict an entry a reader
        is mid-flight on. <code>global: true</code> with <code>ttl: Infinity</code> is an unbounded
        cache — unbounded because the app asked for it, the bound being a number the app knows and
        abide does not.</p>

        <p>A global value over a stream fans one producer out to many readers, and it needs no
        retention to do it. A reader arriving mid-stream gets the run in flight from its start and
        then goes live (2.5), and a reader leaving does not cancel the producer for the ones that
        remain (11.39).</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/keep-the-last-few-values')}>Recent values</a> ·
        <a href={url('/app/know-who-is-calling')}>Auth &amp; principal</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/answer-with-something-other-than-json')}>Response types</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`tags` reach entries across keyed values">
        <Example
            id="cache-tags"
            title="`tags` — a selection that reaches across keyed values"
            summary="Two regions, each with revenue and recent orders behind keyed values that never mention one another. Press one region's button and watch the other stand still."
            files={{
                'Regions.abide': regionsSource,
                'Region.abide': regionSource,
            }}
        >
            <Regions />
        </Example>

        <p><code>tags</code> is the only thing that reaches <strong>across</strong> values. An entry
        carries them, and a selection matches on them. The function form receives the entry's args,
        so a tag can name the <strong>row</strong> rather than the query — which lets
        <code>{'refresh({ tags: [\'region:north\'] })'}</code> reach every entry about that region
        without knowing which values those were. Any other value declaring the same tag joins the
        same selection, in any file.</p>

        <p>So one press moves two numbers that came from two different handlers, and the row
        underneath does not move at all: it declares <code>region:south</code>, and a selection is
        as much what it leaves alone.</p>

        <p>The hand-written arm is where that costs something. An entry table alone cannot answer
        "everything about north", so the tags are a second index beside it, and every entry has to
        be added to both or a later reload quietly misses it.</p>

        <p>A tag is scoped the way a keyed value is: request-local on a server, process-local in a
        browser. One request can never reload another's, and a <code>global</code> value, which
        belongs to no request, is reached from every one.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/decide-when-a-value-reloads')}>Reloading</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/change-something-on-the-server')}>Mutations</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/values/decide-when-a-value-reloads')}>Reloading</a> —
            <code>ttl</code>, <code>invalidate</code>, <code>refresh</code> and selections</li>
            <li><a href={url('/values/slow-down-a-value-that-changes-too-fast')}>Throttle &amp;
            debounce</a> — capping how often an entry revalidates</li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/read-data-without-writing-an-api')}>Reading data</a> —
            <code>GET</code> over a keyed value you named yourself</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
